gs-native-pc (patch 70): GPU-side frame timing (GOW_GS_GPU_PROFILE=1)

`gs_ms` is thread busy time. It cannot separate CPU submission from the GPU still drawing, so every
discussion about "the GS costs 11 ms" was ambiguous: is it the submit path or is the iGPU saturated?

This wires the timestamp queue that already existed (include/runtime/gs/gs_gpu_profile.h, exercised by
ps2xTest) into the backend: two GL_TIMESTAMP counters per frame - opened by the first primitive after a
Present, closed by the next Present - so the pair covers exactly one frame's draws. Results are read four
frames later, and only through GL_QUERY_RESULT_AVAILABLE, so nothing stalls. Off by default: each timestamp
is a driver round trip.

First measurement, 0.5x Asia disc, PS2X_GS_WORKER=1, i3-4170 + HD 4400, 25 s:

    [gs-gpu] frames=4 gpu_ms=5.38 max_ms=10.18
    [gs-gpu] frames=4 gpu_ms=7.94 max_ms=17.08
    [gow-perf] gs_ms=1437 / 5 s over ~125 frames -> 11.5 ms per frame

So the GPU is busy 5-8 ms of an 11.5 ms GS window: roughly half of the GS time is CPU submission, and
the iGPU has ~40 % idle. Consequence for the next step: lowering the internal resolution cannot buy much
here (already known from the discard run), and the queue/expand path is where the GS time actually is.
VU1 (~24 ms per frame in the same window) is still the wall for the frame as a whole.

